NAME
    kermit - transfer a file over the second serial port

SYNOPSIS
    kermit send /path/file      frame a file into packets, send out port 2
    kermit recv /path/file      read packets from port 2, write to a file

DESCRIPTION
    kermit moves a file between the P8X and whatever is on the other end
    of the SECOND serial port (the 2nd ACIA at $FF08/$FF09, added with
    two-mode operation). The console keeps port 1, so a transfer can run
    while you still have a shell -- launch kermit from the Term app, or
    straight from the command line.

    The framing is a minimal Kermit-style block protocol -- a
    fire-and-forward stream, not the full ACK/NAK Kermit. Each packet is

        SEQ  LEN  data[LEN]  CHK

    where SEQ is a wrapping 0-255 counter, LEN is 1..64 payload bytes,
    and CHK is the sum of the payload bytes AND 255. A LEN=0 packet marks
    end of file. `recv` verifies each CHK and prints ?checksum (and exits
    non-zero) if any block is corrupt; otherwise it prints "kermit:
    received". `send` prints "kermit: sent" when the whole file, plus the
    EOF packet, is on the wire.

    The port is polled directly: status bit0 (RDRF) means a byte is
    waiting to be read from $FF09, bit1 (TDRE) means the transmitter is
    ready for the next byte. Both sides must run a matching peer; two
    P8X machines wired port-2 to port-2 can send/recv to each other, and
    the emulator's -2i/-2o file-backed port makes a single machine its
    own peer (that is how the round-trip is tested).

    recv REPLACES any existing file of that name (it deletes first, then
    writes). Give absolute paths -- a /bin command must resolve a bare
    name itself, so lead with '/'.

EXAMPLES
    kermit send /README.TXT     push README.TXT out port 2
    kermit recv /INBOX.DAT      pull the next file in off port 2

    On the emulator, capture a send and play it back into a recv:
        p8xemu ... -2o cap.dat      (kermit send /A.DAT writes cap.dat)
        p8xemu ... -2i cap.dat      (kermit recv /B.DAT reads it back)

NOTES
    kermit is a C-only command for now (no hand-assembled twin yet -- it
    is on the backlog, like `screen`). The C build at /binc/kermit.bin
    and /bin/kermit.bin are the same binary.

